is robust to different error levels and time discretization. We further compare the performance of different models via Wasserstein-2 distance of the SWISS Roll dataset and FIDs of MNIST, which show that CDPMs outperform other SDE models. On the task of CIFAR-10 unconditional generation, we obtain an FID score of 2.47 and an inception score of 10.18 for CDPM, which requires no retraining by transforming the pretrained weights of VE-SDE in [53], surpassing all other SDE models.
Literature review. In the context of generative modeling, DPMs were initiated by [50] (SMLD) and [22] (DDPM) using forward-backward Markov chains. The work [49, 53] unified the previous models via a score-based SDE framework, which also led to deterministic ordinary differential equation (ODE) samplers. Since then the field has exploded, and lots of work has been built upon DPMs and their variants. Examples include DPMs in constrained domains [15, 17, 36, 44], DPMs on manifolds [7, 12, 41], DPMs in graphic models [37], variational DPMs [27, 56] and consistency models [48], just to name a few. Early theory [12, 13, 31] established the convergence of DPMs with exponential dependence on time horizon T and dimension d. Recently, polynomial convergence of various DPMs has been proved for stochastic samplers [3, 10, 20, 32, 33, 35] and deterministic samplers [4, 9, 11, 35], under suitable conditions on the target data distribution.
The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. In Section 2, we provide background on score-based DPMs. Theoretical results for CDPMs are presented in Section 3, and connections to VE are discussed in Section 4. Experiments are reported in Section 5. We conclude with Section 6.
2. Background on Score-based Diffusion Models
2.1. Forward and Backward SDEs. In this work, we explain the formulation of diffusion models based on the Stochastic Differential Equations (SDEs) presented by [53]. Consider a fixed time horizon T , and an SDE-governed forward process {Xt}t∈[0,T ] that describes how data distribution evolves as:
dXt = b(t, Xt)dt + σ(t)dBt, with X0 ∼ pdata(·), (2.1)
where drift coefficient b : R+ × Rd → Rd and diffusion coefficient σ : R+ → R+ are hyper-parameters as part of the design space of diffusion models. Theorectically, some conditions on b(·, ·) and σ(·) are required so that the SDE (2.1) is at least well-defined (see [24, Chapter 5], [55, Section 3.1] for details).
We further denote p(t, ·) be the probability density of Xt (then p(0, ·) = pdata(·) is the data distribution). Diffusion Models consider a time reversal Xt := XT −t for 0 ≤ t ≤ T of the original process Xt for generative sampling. Existing literature on the time reversal of SDEs [1, 21] have proved that X also satisfies an SDE (see proof in Appendix A.), such that
dXt =  ̄b(t, Xt)dt + σ ̄(t)dBt, with X0 ∼ p(T, ·), (2.2)
in which the drift and diffusion coefficient yield a closed-form (we denote ∇ the gradient w.r.p. x):
σ(t) = σ(T − t), b(t, x) = −b(T − t, x) + σ2(T − t)∇ log p(T − t, x). (2.3)
The term ∇ log p(·, ·) is also known as the Stein’s score function. However, scores are unknown, and the procedures or methods to estimate scores are referred as score matching.
